Haemophilia (A/B)
Haematology & Oncology
Spontaneous recurrent haemarthroses, Massive intramuscular haematomas, Prolonged bleeding post-minor trauma, Isolated prolonged aPTT with specific factor deficiency

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-023
Reason for Visit: Sudden, agonizing joint pain and massive swelling in an adult male following trivial movement, requiring urgent evaluation and factor replacement.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a known severe haemophiliac who developed rapid, agonizing swelling in the right knee while walking up stairs. Urgent in-person evaluation was necessary to confirm acute haemarthrosis and facilitate immediate, targeted factor replacement to prevent permanent joint destruction and unremitting pain, which could not wait until the next day.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I just stepped up on the porch, felt a pop, and within an hour my knee blew up to the size of a melon and the pain is excruciating."
History of Present Illness: The 28-year-old male patient has a known, lifelong history of severe Haemophilia A (Factor VIII deficiency). He reports a history of spontaneous, severe, and recurrent haemarthroses typically affecting his knees and ankles. Two hours ago, while casually stepping up a single stair (trivial trauma), he experienced immediate, severe pain and rapid, massive swelling in his right knee. The joint is now agonizingly painful, stiff, and he is entirely unable to bear weight. He denies any head strike, abdominal pain, or other sites of bleeding.
Functional Status: Immobilized on the couch; unable to mobilize due to severe knee pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to access his stored prophylactic factor concentrates located upstairs.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 110 | Temp: 37.0 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Diaphoretic adult in visible agony, rigidly guarding the right leg.
Physical Exam:
Musculoskeletal: The right knee is massively swollen, visibly tense, and held in 20 degrees of fixed flexion. The overlying skin is warm and tightly stretched. The joint is exquisitely tender to even the lightest palpation. Active and passive range of motion is practically zero due to severe pain and mechanical block from the tense effusion (classic acute haemarthrosis). Left knee shows signs of chronic arthropathy (crepitus, thickened synovium).
Skin: No petechiae. Several large, old ecchymoses noted on the forearms.
Neurological: Distal pulses in the right foot are palpable; sensation is intact. No signs of acute compartment syndrome currently.
Environmental Observations: Patient is isolated on the ground floor.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol and Codeine taken 1 hour ago with zero efficacy. Missing scheduled prophylactic Factor VIII infusion.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Haemophilia A - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Severe Haemarthrosis)
Differentials:

Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the sudden, immediate onset following mechanical loading, the lack of systemic high fever, and the patient's classic history of recurrent rapid joint bleeds.

Acute Ligamentous Rupture (ACL tear) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the trivial nature of the trauma (stepping up one stair) and the profound rapidity of the massive, tense swelling (bleeding) characteristic of severe haemophilia.

Gouty Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the specific anatomical location (large joint vs typical 1st MTP), the trauma trigger, and the established genetic diagnosis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, massive right knee haemarthrosis following trivial trauma. In a patient with severe haemophilia, this represents a localized haematological emergency. The rapidly expanding intra-articular bleed causes agonizing pain due to capsular distension and initiates a cycle of severe synovial inflammation that leads to permanent, crippling chronic arthropathy if left untreated. Delaying assessment and intervention until normal clinic hours is absolutely contraindicated, as it would cause unnecessary suffering and irreversible joint damage. The urgent home visit was essential to physically confirm the bleed, retrieve the patient's medication, and facilitate immediate intravenous factor replacement.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: The clinician retrieved the patient's stored recombinant Factor VIII concentrate and assisted the patient in administering an immediate high-dose intravenous bolus to arrest the bleeding. Joint immediately immobilized and elevated with ice packs applied.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Maintain strict, continuous elevation of the right leg above heart level and apply ice packs for 15 minutes every hour. By using gravity and cold to constrict the local blood vessels, you mechanically assist the clotting factor in stopping the bleed and reducing the extreme swelling.

Do not attempt to stand or bear any weight on the right leg for the next 48 hours. By strictly resting the joint, you prevent disrupting the fragile new clot and avoid triggering a secondary, recurrent bleed into the joint space.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to the specialized Haemophilia Treatment Centre for review tomorrow.
DME/Safety (Optional): Crutches requested for future mobilization.
Follow-up: Urgent specialist review at the Haemophilia Centre within 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or call 000 immediately if the pain rapidly worsens despite the factor infusion, if your foot becomes cold, pale, or numb (indicating compromised blood flow), or if you develop a severe headache or vomit.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The physical signs confirm a massive joint bleed; while factor has been given, the joint remains extremely vulnerable to re-bleeding.
Clinical Course: The Factor VIII infusion will stop the active bleeding within the hour. However, the accumulated blood will take days to resorb, and the joint will remain stiff and sore during this time.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Remain completely immobilized on the couch tonight. Do not attempt to walk. Keep the leg elevated continuously.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because your blood lacks a specific protein, a minor strain caused a major bleed into the knee capsule. The intravenous injection directly replaces that missing protein, allowing a solid clot to finally form and stop the destruction of the joint cartilage.
Actionable Steps: Rest strictly. Ice the knee continuously. Contact your haemophilia specialist first thing in the morning.

